  "claims": [
    {
      "id": "c.formula",
      "kind": "framing",
      "text": "This neuron multiplies each input by its weight, adds the bias, then gates the sum with ReLU.",
      "justification": "neuron.c lines 8-14 do exactly this for this one neuron: line 8 and 10 multiply each input by its weight, line 12 adds the two terms and b, and line 14 applies fmax(0.0, z), which is ReLU, g(z) = max{0, z} (Deep Learning §6.3.1). Other neurons may use other activations; this lesson's neuron uses ReLU.",
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Goodfellow, Bengio and Courville, Deep Learning (MIT Press, 2016)",
          "url": "https://www.deeplearningbook.org/contents/mlp.html",
          "clause": "§6.3.1 Rectified Linear Units",
          "quote": "Rectified linear units use the activation function g(z) = max{0, z}."
        },
        {
          "title": "programs/neuron.c",
          "url": "programs/neuron.c",
          "clause": "lines 8-14"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "c.setup",
      "kind": "framing",
      "text": "This neuron has two inputs, 0.8 and 0.4, with weights 1.5 and -1, and a bias of -0.2.",
      "justification": "neuron.c lines 5-7 set x1 = 0.8, w1 = 1.5, x2 = 0.4, w2 = -1.0, b = -0.2; the run prints them with %g as 0.8, 1.5, 0.4, -1 and -0.2.",
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "programs/neuron.c",
          "url": "programs/neuron.c",
          "clause": "lines 5-7"
        },
        {
          "title": "captured run of programs/neuron.c",
          "clause": "stdout lines 1-3"
        }
      ],
      "facts": [
        "f.x1",
        "f.w1",
        "f.x2",
        "f.w2",
        "f.b"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "c.mul",
      "kind": "mechanism",
      "text": "Each input is multiplied by its weight: 0.8 × 1.5 = +1.2 and 0.4 × -1 = -0.4.",
      "justification": "Lines 8 and 10 compute t1 = x1 * w1 and t2 = x2 * w2 in double. In binary64, 0.8 * 1.5 is 1.2000000000000002 and 0.4 * -1.0 is -0.4. %+g with no precision uses P = 6. 1.2000000000000002 has exponent X = 0, so style f with precision P - (X + 1) = 5 gives +1.20000; -0.4 has X = -1, so style f with precision 6 gives -0.400000. Trailing zeros are then removed (7.21.6.1p8, conversion g), giving +1.2 and -0.4 as the run printed.",
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "programs/neuron.c",
          "url": "programs/neuron.c",
          "clause": "lines 8-11"
        },
        {
          "title": "ISO/IEC 9899:2018 (C17), working draft N2176",
          "url": "https://www.open-std.org/jtc1/sc22/wg14/www/docs/n2176.pdf",
          "clause": "§7.21.6.1 ¶8",
          "quote": "if P > X ≥ −4, the conversion is with style f and precision P − (X + 1)."
        },
        {
          "title": "captured run of programs/neuron.c",
          "clause": "stdout lines 1-2"
        }
      ],
      "facts": [
        "f.t1",
        "f.t2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "c.sign",
      "kind": "mechanism",
      "text": "Here the negative weight -1 turns the positive input 0.4 into a negative term, -0.4.",
      "justification": "Line 10 computes t2 = x2 * w2 = 0.4 * -1.0; the product of a positive and a negative number is negative, and the run printed -0.4 for t2.",
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "captured run of programs/neuron.c",
          "clause": "stdout line 2"
        }
      ],
      "facts": [
        "f.t2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "c.add",
      "kind": "mechanism",
      "text": "The neuron adds the two terms and the bias: 1.2 - 0.4 - 0.2 = 0.6. That sum is z.",
      "justification": "Line 12 computes z = t1 + t2 + b, left to right in double: 1.2000000000000002 + -0.4 = 0.8000000000000002, then + -0.2 = 0.6000000000000001, which %g prints as 0.6 (third line of the run, after z =).",
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "programs/neuron.c",
          "url": "programs/neuron.c",
          "clause": "line 12"
        },
        {
          "title": "captured run of programs/neuron.c",
          "clause": "stdout line 3"
        }
      ],
      "facts": [
        "f.z"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "c.relu",
      "kind": "definition",
      "text": "ReLU outputs max(0, z): a positive z passes through unchanged, and a z of 0 or less gives 0.",
      "justification": "The rectified linear unit is defined as ReLU(z) = max(0, z). The program computes it with fmax(0.0, z) (line 14); C17 7.12.12.2: fmax returns the larger of its arguments, and for z = 0 both arguments equal 0.",
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "ISO/IEC 9899:2018 (C17), working draft N2176",
          "url": "https://www.open-std.org/jtc1/sc22/wg14/www/docs/n2176.pdf",
          "clause": "§7.12.12.2 ¶2",
          "quote": "The fmax functions return the maximum numeric value of their arguments."
        },
        {
          "title": "programs/neuron.c",
          "clause": "line 14"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "c.out",
      "kind": "mechanism",
      "text": "Here z is 0.6, which is positive, so the output a is 0.6.",
      "justification": "fmax(0.0, 0.6000000000000001) is 0.6000000000000001, printed with %g as 0.6 on the last line of the run.",
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "programs/neuron.c",
          "url": "programs/neuron.c",
          "clause": "lines 14-15"
        },
        {
          "title": "captured run of programs/neuron.c",
          "clause": "stdout line 4"
        }
      ],
      "facts": [
        "f.a"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "c.toy",
      "kind": "framing",
      "text": "The numbers are toy values with no units. The program computes them in double and prints them with %g or %+g, which show at most 6 significant digits.",
      "justification": "programs/neuron.c declares every quantity as double and prints with %g / %+g (lines 9, 11, 13, 15); C17 7.21.6.1p8: for g, the precision is the number of significant digits, 6 when omitted, and trailing zeros are removed.",
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "programs/neuron.c",
          "clause": "lines 5-15"
        },
        {
          "title": "ISO/IEC 9899:2018 (C17), working draft N2176",
          "url": "https://www.open-std.org/jtc1/sc22/wg14/www/docs/n2176.pdf",
          "clause": "§7.21.6.1 ¶8"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "c.pause",
      "kind": "framing",
      "text": "Which answer do you predict? Pick one of the three before the answer plays.",
      "justification": "Retrieval prompt required by the learner workspace's predict-first contract; it invites the learner to commit to one of the question's options before the answer is shown, and states no fact about C.",
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Learner workspace contract (AGENTS.md: ask one bounded prediction, wait for the attempt)",
          "clause": "Teaching"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "c.round",
      "kind": "caveat",
      "text": "In double, z here is not exactly 0.6, but %g rounds it to 6 significant digits and prints 0.6.",
      "justification": "0.8, 1.5, 0.4 and 0.2 have no exact binary64 form; 0.8 * 1.5 evaluates to the double just above 1.2, so t1 + t2 + b is the double just above 0.6 (0.6000000000000001 in shortest round-trip form), and z == 0.6 is false. %g with the default precision 6 prints it as 0.6 (C17 7.21.6.1p8; Annex F binary64).",
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "ISO/IEC 9899:2018 (C17), working draft N2176",
          "url": "https://www.open-std.org/jtc1/sc22/wg14/www/docs/n2176.pdf",
          "clause": "§7.21.6.1 ¶8"
        },
        {
